cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use pyflightstream, please cite it using this metadata."
title: "pyflightstream: version-aware, didactic Python driver for the FlightStream panel-method solver"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Neves
    given-names: Geovana
license: MIT
# The version and the release date are set together, always. Between
# releases this file carries a development version and NO date-released,
# because a development tree has no release date; it once stated the
# v0.3.0 date beside version 0.4.0.dev0, so a citation generated from
# that tree asserted a release date for an artifact that was never
# released (REV010-015). This is a RELEASE tree: the version carries no
# .dev0 and states date-released, which is the pair the
# guard in tests/tier1_offline/test_metadata_currency.py enforces in both directions.
# The next commit to move the pair is the post-tag one that opens the next
# development cycle, and it sets them the other way with this sentence; it is written as a statement about the
# tree rather than as a rule so that it cannot quietly stop describing
# the file it heads. It has now stopped TWICE, once in each direction:
# on 2026-08-11 the version moved to .dev0 and this paragraph still
# said RELEASE, and on 2026-09-04 the v0.12.0 release commit set the
# version and the date and left the paragraph saying DEVELOPMENT. Both
# were found by a review reading the prose against the fields, which is
# the residual the wording was chosen to make VISIBLE rather than
# remove: the guard reads the two fields and cannot read a sentence.
# Twice is a pattern, and the honest reading of it is that a paragraph
# a human must remember to flip will keep not flipping. THREE TIMES now:
# on 2026-09-10 the v0.15.0 release set the version and the date and left
# this paragraph saying DEVELOPMENT, and the 0.16.0.dev0 bump the next
# day found it that way and flipped it to RELEASE and then immediately
# back. The flip that follows the bump in the same commit is the only
# one that has ever worked. THE TALLY, kept separate because merging the two
# into one number is what made the first writing of this sentence
# unreconstructable: THREE failures are recorded above, and the version,
# the date and this sentence have moved together in one commit NINETEEN
# times: the 0.16.0.dev0 bump of 2026-09-11, the v0.16.0 RELEASE commit of
# the same day, the 0.17.0.dev0 bump, the v0.17.0 RELEASE commit of
# 2026-09-13, the 0.18.0.dev0 bump, the v0.18.0 RELEASE commit of
# 2026-09-14, which was the SIXTH, the 0.18.1.dev0 bump of the same day, the
# SEVENTH, the v0.18.1 RELEASE commit, the EIGHTH, the 0.19.0.dev0 bump, the
# NINTH, the v0.19.0 RELEASE commit, the TENTH, the 0.20.0.dev0 bump, the
# ELEVENTH, the v0.20.0 RELEASE commit, the TWELFTH, the 0.20.1.dev0 bump,
# the THIRTEENTH, the v0.20.1 RELEASE commit, the FOURTEENTH, the
# 0.21.0.dev0 bump, the FIFTEENTH, the v0.21.0 RELEASE commit of
# 2026-09-16, the SIXTEENTH, the 0.21.1.dev0 bump, the SEVENTEENTH, and
# the v0.21.1 RELEASE commit of 2026-09-16, the EIGHTEENTH, and the
# 0.22.0.dev0 bump, the NINETEENTH, which was owed from the moment that tag
# was cut: it was not made then, so the tree sat at the released identity
# while carrying code that release does not have, which is the thing this
# pair exists to prevent (the technical-writing lens, FIX-0212). THIS
# COMMIT IS THE v0.25.1 RELEASE, and the paragraph is re-stated with it
# because a tally naming a bump three releases old as "this" one is the
# same staleness from the other side (the independent review of GitHub
# main, 2026-09-22). A FOURTH
# failure was caught by
# the verification lens rather than by the guard: the release commit advanced
# the pair and left this sentence claiming it was the second move and the
# first in a release, which is the exact staleness the tally exists to count
# and which no guard can see, because the guard reads the two FIELDS and this
# is prose about a commit.
# A FIFTH failure, recorded because it is the same shape from a third angle:
# on 2026-09-13 the technical-writing lens read this block on the commit that
# paid the v0.17.0 archive row and found the tally still naming a
# 0.17.0.dev0 bump as "this" one on a RELEASE tree. It was registered to the
# owner rather than repaired, because how much of this history to keep is a
# judgement and not a wording correction, and it is repaired here only
# because the bump that repairs it is the commit that also moves the pair.
# The archive DOIs below are one per release from v0.3.0, newest first,
# with the concept DOI last. THREE are absent, not two. v0.1.0 and v0.2.0:
# v0.2.0 was the first release to archive and its version DOI was not
# recorded here at the time. And v0.14.0, whose archive did not exist when
# it was last measured against the service's own API; the Owed section of
# CHANGELOG.md carries that and says what to re-run. This sentence listed
# two and the list skipped three, which the verification lens caught. The
# concept DOI resolves to the newest archived version either way, so
# nothing points at a release that cannot be cited. v0.1.0 and v0.2.0 are a
# gap in this list; v0.14.0 is a gap in the archive itself.
# THE COUNT EXCLUDES A ROW STILL INSIDE THE WINDOW between a tag and the
# commit after it, and that is why it is reconstructible rather than
# true-on-the-day-it-was-written: at the v0.17.0 release commit FOUR rows
# were absent and this sentence said three, because a row in that window
# is not a category the sentence had a word for. It has one now, and the
# Owed section of CHANGELOG.md is where such a row lives while it waits.
# A version DOI is minted from the GitHub RELEASE and not from the tag,
# and creating that release is a MANUAL step, so the identifier is always
# recorded one commit AFTER the tag it names -- and a tag whose release
# nobody created is archived nowhere at all, which is what happened to
# v0.17.0 for a day. Older ones
# stay, because those releases did happen and are still what a citation
# of them should resolve to.
